<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Mandela returns five weeks after his first visit. The PSMA PET/CT two weeks ago showed uptake only in the prostate, with no spread to lymph nodes or bone. He had his DXA scan this morning and then saw Dr. van Zyl of radiation oncology, who went through observation, radiotherapy with short-term androgen deprivation, and surgery with him. He chose radiotherapy to the prostate with six months of androgen deprivation. He starts bicalutamide today and will have his first leuprolide injection at the radiation oncology clinic in one week. Before starting, he was told the expected effects of androgen deprivation (hot flashes, tiredness, loss of libido and erections, weight gain, bone loss, and changes in mood or memory) and how they are monitored. His urinary symptoms are unchanged and mild. He has no bone pain, weight loss, or breathlessness. He starts vitamin D today for the low level found at the first visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Staging</td><td>PSMA PET/CT: disease confined to the prostate (N0 M0)</td></tr><tr><td>Decision</td><td>Radiotherapy with six months of androgen deprivation, after radiation oncology consultation today</td></tr><tr><td>Starting today</td><td>Bicalutamide 50 mg daily for four weeks; cholecalciferol 1000 units daily</td></tr><tr><td>Next step</td><td>First leuprolide injection in one week at radiation oncology</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

