  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Prostate cancer, unfavorable intermediate risk, cT1c N0 M0 (C61)</content>. Staging is complete and shows localized disease. The decision for radiotherapy with six months of androgen deprivation follows the radiation oncology consultation and the guidelines for unfavorable intermediate risk in a fit man of his age. [S]<list><item>Bicalutamide 50 mg daily for four weeks from today.</item><item>Leuprolide 22.5 mg in one week, then every twelve weeks for a second dose.</item><item>Fiducial markers and planning scans in about twelve weeks; radiotherapy to start about three months after leuprolide.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Androgen deprivation therapy: baseline (Z79.899)</content>. Baseline testosterone 412 ng/dL, hemoglobin A1c 5.5 percent, LDL 112 mg/dL, and blood pressure 130/76 mm Hg. DXA shows low bone mass at the femoral neck (T-score -1.4) with a normal spine (-0.6). His ten-year hip fracture risk is below the 3 percent treatment threshold, so no bone drug is needed now. Vitamin D was 26 ng/mL. [S]<list><item>Cholecalciferol 1000 units daily from today.</item><item>Calcium from food, about 1000 to 1200 mg a day; walking and resistance exercise.</item><item>PSA, testosterone, and vitamin D in three months; DXA again in one to two years.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>PSA before treatment</td><td>At diagnosis, then every 3 to 6 months on androgen deprivation</td><td>Up to date</td><td>6.4 ng/mL at the first visit; next on androgen deprivation in three months</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Testosterone</td><td>Baseline, then every 3 to 6 months on androgen deprivation (target below 50 ng/dL)</td><td>Up to date</td><td>412 ng/dL at the first visit, before treatment; recheck in three months</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hemoglobin A1c or fasting glucose, and lipid panel</td><td>Baseline and yearly on androgen deprivation</td><td>Up to date</td><td>A1c 5.5 percent, glucose 94 mg/dL, LDL 112 mg/dL at the first visit; next in one year</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Calcium and 25-hydroxyvitamin D</td><td>Baseline and periodically on androgen deprivation</td><td>Due</td><td>Calcium 9.4 mg/dL and vitamin D 26 ng/mL at the first visit; supplement starts today; vitamin D recheck in three months</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Bone density (DXA)</td><td>Baseline before androgen deprivation, then every 1 to 2 years</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Femoral neck T-score -1.4 (low bone mass); lumbar spine -0.6 (normal); hip fracture risk below the treatment threshold</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>PSMA PET/CT staging</td><td>Once, for unfavorable intermediate or high risk</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Two weeks ago: no nodal, bone, or visceral disease</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure and cardiovascular assessment</td><td>Every visit on androgen deprivation</td><td>Completed today</td><td>130/76 mm Hg; no chest pain or breathlessness; baseline before treatment</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Distress and depression screen (PHQ-2)</td><td>At diagnosis and at intervals</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-2 score 0</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Urinary symptoms (IPSS)</td><td>Baseline before treatment, then every follow-up visit</td><td>Up to date</td><td>IPSS 7 (mild) at the first visit; no change today</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Sexual function</td><td>Baseline before treatment and periodically</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Reviewed before androgen deprivation; the expected effects were explained</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Cognition</td><td>Periodically on androgen deprivation</td><td>Due</td><td>Cognitive screen at the annual wellness visit in about eight weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Hot flashes and fatigue</td><td>Every visit on androgen deprivation</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Androgen deprivation not yet started; counseled on what to expect</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Testosterone flare protection</td><td>When starting a GnRH agonist</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Bicalutamide starts today, one week before leuprolide, and continues for four weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Radiation oncology</td><td>Once, then through treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen today; recommendations in the consultation note</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Physical activity, calcium intake, and no tobacco</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Walking every morning; resistance exercise added; dietary calcium reviewed</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Reach an informed, shared decision on treatment after hearing every reasonable option, including observation. Outcome by 2001-04-14: Radiotherapy with six months of androgen deprivation chosen at the radiation oncology consultation [S]</item><item>Complete six months of androgen deprivation and the course of radiotherapy with side effects managed. In progress; target 2001-10-27 [S]</item><item>Protect bone health on androgen deprivation: baseline DXA, vitamin D of 30 ng/mL or more, dietary calcium, and weight-bearing exercise. In progress; target 2001-07-14 [S]</item><item>PSA falling after radiotherapy, with a written surveillance plan. In progress; target 2001-10-27 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Prostate Cancer (2026); Eastham JA, et al. Clinically Localized Prostate Cancer: AUA/ASTRO Guideline, Part I: Introduction, Risk Assessment, Staging, and Risk-Based Management. J Urol 2022;208(1):10-18 (2022); Skolarus TA, et al. American Cancer Society prostate cancer survivorship care guidelines. CA Cancer J Clin 2014;64(4):225-249 (2014); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, prostate cancer monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Leuprolide at radiation oncology in one week. Annual wellness visit in eight weeks. Primary care follow-up in three months with PSA, testosterone, and vitamin D. Next scheduled visit 2001-06-09: subsequent annual wellness visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
